  "strategy": "<section title=\"Approach and rationale\">\n    We propose a novel approach combining periodic symmetry with stochastic perturbation and deterministic optimization. Unlike previous methods that focused on local refinement, this strategy explores the full periodic nature of the optimal solution space. The key ideas are:\n    1. The optimal h function likely has periodic symmetry due to the circular convolution-like nature of the problem\n    2. We generate diverse candidate solutions by perturbing regular patterns derived from the provided parent solution\n    3. We use both gradient-based optimization within promising regions and a dedicated repair mechanism to enforce the integral constraint\n    4. Multiple resolutions (n_points = 280, 560, 1120) are considered with a fallback strategy for time-constrained scenarios\n\n    The main bottleneck addressed is the tendency of previous methods to get trapped in narrow regions near the parent solution. By systematically exploring periodic structures across resolutions, we can find more globally optimal candidates.\n  </section>\n\n  <section title=\"Mathematical specifications\">\n    <subsection title=\"Objective and constraints\">\n      We maintain the original problem formulation with h(x) \u2208 [0,1], \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1, and minimize:\n        C\u2085 = dx \u00b7 max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n      The objective is evaluated via:\n        dx = 2.0/n_points\n        corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n        C\u2085 = dx\u00b7np.max(corr)\n\n      Additional terms:\n        Signal length = 2\u00b7n_points - 1\n        Integral constraint: \u2211h_i\u00b7dx = 1 \u21d2 \u2211h_i = n_points/2\n        Bounds: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n    </subsection>\n\n    <subsection title=\"Algorithm design\">\n      Key mathematical components:\n        - Periodicity preservation: Maintain k-periodic components where k divides n_points\n        - Constraint enforcement: RepairVector(y, target_sum) \u2192 Ensure \u2211y = target_sum with bounds\n        - Resolution mapping: ScaleSolution(h, scale_factor) \u2192 Linear/interpolation-based extension\n      Optimization formulation (for each resolution):\n        minimize C\u2085\n        subject to:\n          \u2211h_i = target_sum = n_points/2\n          0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n        We use project_feasible(y, target_sum) as the repair function:\n          target_sum = n_points/2\n          while \u2209 [0.999\u00b7target_sum, 1.001\u00b7target_sum]:\n              mid = (lo+hi)/2, s=np.clip(y-mid,0,1).sum()\n              if s>target_sum: lo=mid\n              else: hi=mid\n      The selection criteria is a combination of candidate value and rank across multiple resolutions.\n    </subsection>\n  </section>\n\n  <section title=\"Implementation procedure\">\n    <step title=\"Input handling and fallback\">\n      1. Validate input including n_points_seed and parent solution\n      2. Set time management parameters:\n        time_per_resolution = [800, 325, 215]  # s for 1,2,3x resolutions\n        eval_budget(res0) = ceil(time_per_resolution[0] / 0.0000017)  # Approx 470k evals at n=280\n      3. Initialize population:\n        population_size = 150 (divisible by 3 for diversity)\n        diversity_threshold = 0.9 (pairwise distance correlation)\n      4. Check if initial_h_values is available and valid\n    </step>\n\n    <step title=\"Periodicity-based population generation\">\n      1. If parent available:\n        core_pattern = h_parent[:n0_periods]  # Use first n0_periods to define periodicity\n      2. Generate populations for each resolution:\n        For scale_factor in [1,2,4]: # 280,560,1120\n          population = []\n          mean_vector = core_pattern\n          for i in range(population_size):\n            if i % 4 == 0:\n                noisy_core = mean_vector + 0.1*randn(len(mean_vector))\n                noise_repaired = RepairVector(noisy_core, target_sum)\n            elif i % 4 == 1:\n                shifted_core = np.roll(mean_vector, i//4)\n                shifted_repaired = RepairVector(shifted_core, target_sum)\n            else:\n                Generate random vector with sum\u2248target_sum\n            population.append(processed_vector)\n      </step>\n\n    <step title=\"Multi-resolution optimization\">\n      1. For each resolution level (280,560,1120):\n        a. Solve constrained optimization:\n            method = 'SLSQP'\n            vars = current_pop\n            constraints = [{'type':'eq','fun':ComputeIntegral}]\n            obj = VectorObjective(dx)\n            Use RepairVector as fallback repair\n        b. Apply dimension reduction techniques\n        c. Track best candidate across each level\n      </step>\n\n    <step title=\"Resolution mapping\">\n      def ScaleSolution(h_original, original_n, new_n):\n        # Linear indexing preserving periodicity\n        scale_factor = new_n/original_n\n        if scale_factor.is_integer():\n            step = int(original_n/scale_factor)\n            h_scaled = np.interp(np.arange(new_n)*step, np.linspace(0,1,original_n), h_original)\n        else:\n            # Bi-linear interpolation of periodic components\n            h_scaled = np.interp(np.linspace(0,1,new_n), \n                              np.linspace(0,1,original_n), \n                              h_original)\n        return RepairVector(h_scaled, new_n/2)\n    </step>\n\n    <step title=\"Termination handling\">\n      \u2022 If time expired: Return best found candidate\n      \u2022 If NaN/Inf detected: Repair and retry\n      \u2022 Maintain diversity budget of 0.75 between generations\n    </step>\n  </section>\n\n  <step title=\"Search choices and variations\">\n    <subsection title=\"Resolution adjustment\">\n      <item title=\"Deserialization factor\">Use factors: [1,2,4] (equivalent to n_points = 280,560,1120)</item>\n      <item title=\"Periodicity weight\">Parameter controls: p\u03c0 \u2208 [0.5,0.9]</item>\n      <item title=\"Dimension expansion\">Decide per seed: expand_dims(seed) = (seed%7) from {0,1,2}</item>\n    </subsection>\n    <subsection title=\"Convergence criteria\">\n      <item title=\"Evaluation halt\">stop_evals(n_evals) = max(500000, int(query_hardware('eval_time_msec')*time_constant))</item>\n      <item title=\"Diversity maintenance\">Distance threshold: d_threshold = 0.1 / sqrt(n_points)</item>\n    </subsection>\n  </step>\n\n  <step title=\"Computation budget and robustness\">\n    <item title=\"Time estimation\">Average eval time: t_eval ~= 2.2e-4 seconds (n=560)</item>\n    <item title=\"Peak memory\">Reserve 3\u00d7resolution vectors: 3*560*8/1e6 = 13.5 MB/level</item>\n    <item title=\"Stall detection\">No improvement > 1000 evals \u2192 perturbation phase</item>\n    <item title>\"Fallback construction\">Initial solution: SymmetricScaled(parent, scale=2) if available</item>\n  </step>\n\n  <step title=\"Validation and return contract\">\n    <item title=\"Feasibility checks\">Verify 0\u2264h_i\u22641 and |ComputeIntegral(h)-target|<1e-6</item>\n    <item title=\"Objective reevaluation\">Compute C\u2085 using np.correlate with mode='full'</item>\n    <item title=\"Solution comparison\">Rank candidates by C\u2085, tie-breaking by lower resolution</item>\n    <item title>\"Fallback if needed\">Provide solution for n_points=560 if optimization exceeds budget</item>\n  </step>",
